/**
 * Joins the elements of the provided {@code Object} array into a single String
 * using the specified separator.
 *
 * <p>This method allows specifying a sub-array range using {@code startIndex}
 * and {@code endIndex}. Elements within this range are converted to their
 * String representation using {@code toString()} and then concatenated.
 * Null elements within the specified range are ignored and not included in the
 * joined string. If the separator is null, an empty string is used as the separator.</p>
 *
 * <p>Example:</p>
 * <pre>
 * StringUtils.join(new Object[] {"a", null, "b"}, ",", 0, 3)   = "a,b"
 * StringUtils.join(new Object[] {"a", "b", "c"}, ",", 0, 2)   = "a,b"
 * StringUtils.join(new Object[] {"a", "b", "c"}, ",", 1, 3)   = "b,c"
 * StringUtils.join(new Object[] {"a", "b", "c"}, null, 0, 3) = "abc"
 * StringUtils.join(new Object[] {"a", "b", "c"}, ",", 0, 0)   = ""
 * StringUtils.join(new Object[] {"a", "b", "c"}, ",", 1, 1)   = ""
 * StringUtils.join(null, ",", 0, 3)                         = null
 * </pre>
 *
 * @param array      the array of objects to join, may be null
 * @param separator  the separator string to use, null treated as ""
 * @param startIndex the first index to start joining from (inclusive)
 * @param endIndex   the index to stop joining at (exclusive)
